WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:03.962
Over the last couple of weeks,

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:08.466
you might have seen photos
like this flooding your social media feed.

00:00:08.758 --> 00:00:11.386
These are the snapshots from Burning Man,
which,

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:15.473
if you don't know, is the notorious mega
cultural event

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:20.103
held in the desert of Nevada,
which they call Black Rock City.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:21.688
They build stuff.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:22.981
They burn stuff.

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:27.485
And over the timespan of nine days,
these group of people live together

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:32.866
and they function together
around the ten core principles of radical

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:37.328
inclusion, gifting,
commodification, radical self-reliance,

00:00:37.495 --> 00:00:41.916
radical self-expression,
communal effort, civic responsibility,

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:46.212
leaving no trace participation
and immediacy.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.965
A portion of this video
is sponsored by Soundcore.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.176
More on them later in this video.

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:55.430
It started in 1986 with just 20 people.

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:59.476
But it's been growing by the thousands
every single year.

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:03.354
And this year it held about 80,000 people.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:07.567
But throughout Burning Man's history,
there's been multiple accidents,

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:13.156
deaths, arrests, as well as some
really horrific stories of sexual assault.

00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:17.535
And in spite of their principle
of radical inclusion,

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:20.622
the desert environment
doesn't really seem exactly

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:24.042
like the most hospitable place
for people with mobility issues.

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:28.046
Some of the installation
seemed to not meet any kind of safety

00:01:28.046 --> 00:01:32.300
regulation, and in fact,
throughout its history there's

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.721
been concern run ins with law enforcement
and the Bureau of Land Management.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.934
And with this type of massive growth
every year.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.479
They've been constantly dealing
with functional concerns,

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:48.650
traffic safety concerns, problems
with scale and accessibility.

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:52.070
So how has this event continued on

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.240
for so long and why is it growing so fast

00:01:55.657 --> 00:02:01.329
and how did it go from this to this?

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:02.831
What's up, guys?

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:07.293
My name is Danny and I am an architect
in beautiful Vancouver, B.C.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:08.837
And when I started looking

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:12.715
at the history of Burning Man,
I was actually really shocked.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:16.553
And I think it's a really fascinating case
study from a design and urban

00:02:16.553 --> 00:02:17.428
planning point of view.

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:21.766
This place has no water, no vegetation,
and the temperatures,

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:26.771
they get up to 100 degrees Fahrenheit
and they drop to freezing at night.

00:02:26.938 --> 00:02:30.984
And they have windstorms
that kind of cover everything in dust.

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:35.947
And despite all of that, every summer,
these group of people assemble

00:02:35.947 --> 00:02:39.701
a small city,
and after nine days, they disassemble it

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:43.788
and they leave zero trace
of any human presence.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.750
I think Burning
Man is a really interesting example of how

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:52.672
constrains, whether it's legal
or functional or site specific,

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:58.094
how these constraints have shaped a city
into something that's so successful.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:02.140
In fact,
the very first plan of Burning Man

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:08.313
was developed in 1992 for a population
of just 600, just to satisfy

00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:13.067
the permitting requirements in 1996
with a population of 8000.

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:17.113
The added avenues for the very first time
and a second circle.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.200
And in order
to preserve the view of the burning Man,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:25.538
they created a zone called No Man's Land,
lined on either sides with theme camps

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:30.376
and which were and to this day
are kind of the heart of Burning Man.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.756
It can be anything from services,
entertainment or other interactive

00:03:34.756 --> 00:03:41.137
experiences organized by the people,
for the people as gifts to the community.

00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:44.224
Still, with more growth,
they were constantly

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:47.227
having issues with traffic, chaos, sprawl.

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:50.855
And every year they were losing

00:03:50.855 --> 00:03:53.942
the intimacy
of the original camping circle,

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:59.322
which led to accidents
and a sense of isolation and conflict

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:13.920
between people.

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:16.047
Shut up.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.384
Here, try this.

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:21.386
What is this?

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:23.429
It's sound course. Sleep at home.

00:04:23.513 --> 00:04:28.059
It's got enhanced noise blocking system,
and it's got a massive sound library

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:29.936
to help you improve your sleep.

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:34.065
It's also got a four point noise
masking system and it blocks out

00:04:34.065 --> 00:04:40.154
35 decibels of noise, which is 15 decibels
more than your standard earbuds.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.198
But I'm a side sleeper.

00:04:42.198 --> 00:04:43.116
No problem.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:48.121
It's lightweight,
so it stays in your ears very snugly.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.749
Oh. Oh.

00:04:51.749 --> 00:04:54.544
Check the link in the description
for a limited time offer.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.130
Whoa.

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:01.509
In 1997, Burning Man took place

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.679
in a different location
near Kuala Pae Valley.

00:05:04.846 --> 00:05:06.306
This is when they recruited Rod

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:10.601
Garratt, who's a landscape architect
by training, and he put together

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:13.813
the plans and documents
to meet the regulations of the county.

00:05:13.896 --> 00:05:18.067
The permitting requirements,
if for some to include roads and street

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:22.572
names and urban amenities
and due to the constraints of the site.

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:26.784
Also, in order to condense everything
within the property line in naturally

00:05:26.784 --> 00:05:30.538
developed this arc that's spread out
laterally from the center.

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:34.542
The year after, in 1998,
when they returned to their home

00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:38.546
in Nevada, they kept a lot of the aspect
from their experiment.

00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:43.843
In 1997, they decided to form the city
into a concentric circle

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:47.138
around a geographic center
where you could see the men.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:49.307
Just conceptually.

00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:52.935
I really love this idea of a moving city.

00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:56.105
Like it's a city that morphed itself
to meet

00:05:56.105 --> 00:05:59.984
the constraints of this new site
and learn from its conditions.

00:05:59.984 --> 00:06:03.821
And then when it came back home,
have become a better version of itself.

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:07.116
By the early 2000, Burning Man had grown

00:06:07.116 --> 00:06:11.454
to 25,000 people
officially the size of the town.

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:16.709
And I think here
you can see one of the most understated

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:20.922
and fascinating things
about the evolution of Black Rock City.

00:06:21.005 --> 00:06:27.345
So this was the point where a lot of the
problems really started to get amplified.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.808
There were staff disruptions
and splinter groups and safety concerns,

00:06:31.808 --> 00:06:36.854
and the planners of Black Rock City
identified that a lot of these problems

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:42.568
can be traced back to alienation
leading to the loss of human connection.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:48.032
So they decided to ban vehicles
except for the approved art vehicles.

00:06:48.116 --> 00:06:50.952
They tried to make the settlement
a little bit more walkable.

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:55.039
And the proposal to the plan
was to instead of having this thick

00:06:55.123 --> 00:06:59.293
band of theme camps in the prime, inside
ring of the city,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.588
that these camps would be distributed
throughout the plan.

00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:07.343
This meant that they had to break down
these monolithic sections, and when they

00:07:07.343 --> 00:07:12.682
did find some success with the strategy,
they started interjecting more

00:07:12.849 --> 00:07:17.019
and more of these theme camps
into the rest of the site year after year.

00:07:17.145 --> 00:07:20.481
But of course, it's
when you start getting these mixed use

00:07:20.481 --> 00:07:23.484
scenarios where you start
to have more obvious issues.

00:07:23.526 --> 00:07:28.197
For example, having like a dance party
themed camp next to like a quiet

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:31.868
relaxation zone,
that could be problematic.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:37.165
And that's actually why so many cities
are zoned with very distinct uses.

00:07:37.206 --> 00:07:42.879
This requires a lot more granular planning
from everyone involved.

00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:45.923
So Roger Garrett,
he started implementing zoning

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:48.968
to separate the potentially clashing uses.

00:07:49.385 --> 00:07:53.598
And months before the event,
people have to submit the plans

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:55.516
for their camps for approval.

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:58.561
There is fairly strict guidelines,
it seems like, for

00:07:58.561 --> 00:08:01.606
the design of these camps
with like dimensions

00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:06.903
and potentially conflicting uses
as well as pretty much everything else.

00:08:06.944 --> 00:08:11.407
But can this really solve
the problem of safety and accessibility?

00:08:11.574 --> 00:08:14.452
Throughout Burning Man's history,
there's been numerous

00:08:14.452 --> 00:08:17.330
deaths and near fatal accidents.

00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:19.457
There's open fires.

00:08:19.457 --> 00:08:23.211
There's art vehicles,
and the art installations are claimable.

00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:27.882
And of course, you can't compare
Burning Man to a real city.

00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:31.677
You know, people only come here
for nine days, and after that

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:34.931
they go back to the safety
and comforts of their own homes.

00:08:35.014 --> 00:08:38.726
And none of these are like
real permanent structures.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:44.232
But just to put things in perspective,
the safety regulations of the building

00:08:44.232 --> 00:08:47.777
code is so, so, so, so strict

00:08:48.319 --> 00:08:51.614
that, for example,
something that I just came across

00:08:51.614 --> 00:08:55.535
the other day with my project is let's say
we have an existing building, right?

00:08:55.701 --> 00:09:00.915
And a fire exit this way and a fire exit
this way and a path this way.

00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:03.000
And this is like a grassy area.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.754
And so we decided to put windows
right here to give a little bit

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:09.006
more daylight into this admin space.

00:09:09.507 --> 00:09:12.843
But the city wanted us to put
water curtains,

00:09:12.843 --> 00:09:15.763
which are kind of like sprinklers
above these windows.

00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:20.476
And they wanted a new path here
because in case there's a fire,

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.814
we have to assume that people
are going to come out of the building

00:09:25.106 --> 00:09:28.192
and they're going to be like, Oh, my God,
where do I go?

00:09:28.609 --> 00:09:30.820
I must follow this path.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.824
But there is only one path,
and we have to assume

00:09:34.824 --> 00:09:37.910
that they're either going to go this way
or this way.

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:42.748
And if they go this way,
there's like a massive raging fire

00:09:42.748 --> 00:09:46.544
that's going to shoot out this window
and people are going to get hurt.

00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:52.216
But in reality and in common sense,
when people leave a burning building,

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:54.802
they're going to be walking out
in all directions

00:09:54.802 --> 00:09:59.765
and they're not going to care whether it's
a paved path or if it's a grassy area.

00:09:59.974 --> 00:10:02.727
So this is like one of the few examples.

00:10:02.727 --> 00:10:06.856
But anyways,
what I'm trying to say is the building

00:10:06.856 --> 00:10:09.859
code forces us
to put these safety precautions in place

00:10:09.859 --> 00:10:13.112
that sometimes go a little bit
beyond common sense.

00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:18.117
And they really force us to design
for the most extreme scenarios.

00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:21.412
And every time they print a new building
code, it's built

00:10:21.412 --> 00:10:25.708
upon the accidents or like the lawsuits
from the years before.

00:10:25.833 --> 00:10:30.880
But the point of all of this
is to provide a safe and equitable place

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:36.260
for everyone, you know, no matter what
your physical or mental capacities are.

00:10:36.385 --> 00:10:41.057
Because if you don't put in rules
like this, you'll get designs like this.

00:10:41.641 --> 00:10:45.811
So doesn't this and this kind of go

00:10:45.811 --> 00:10:49.565
against one of their core principles
of radical inclusion?

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:53.486
The weird thing is, even in
probably one of the most

00:10:53.527 --> 00:10:57.823
inhospitable conditions,
there's actually quite a large community

00:10:57.823 --> 00:11:02.787
of people with disabilities at Burning Man
that actually come back year after year.

00:11:02.995 --> 00:11:07.124
They have what's called a mobility camp
since 2012, whose camp

00:11:07.124 --> 00:11:11.170
motto is radical inclusion
through radical accessibility.

00:11:11.295 --> 00:11:12.963
And they have things like wheelchair

00:11:12.963 --> 00:11:16.717
accessible camping power
for medical equipment.

00:11:16.759 --> 00:11:22.056
They are volunteers and they take out
a tractor and like a 20 foot accessible

00:11:22.056 --> 00:11:25.643
trailer rig out onto the desert
three or four times a day,

00:11:25.893 --> 00:11:29.772
so that people who can't bike or walk,
they can still experience the art

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:33.067
by which they do
make it very clear on the Burning

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:37.446
Man website the reality of the conditions
at the event.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:41.617
And they really stress
that, you know, even though

00:11:41.742 --> 00:11:45.788
there's a big support network, everyone,
you know, no matter what

00:11:45.788 --> 00:11:50.918
your physical capabilities
is expected to live by the principles

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.755
of participation
and radical self-reliance.

00:11:54.964 --> 00:11:58.843
And I think, weirdly enough, that's

00:11:58.926 --> 00:12:02.680
one of the reasons
why they feel empowered and accepted.

00:12:02.805 --> 00:12:08.018
They're not just seen as helpless or seem
just for their disabilities.

00:12:08.018 --> 00:12:11.480
They're just another burner
or that's what they call themselves

00:12:11.981 --> 00:12:40.634
that use the art to express themselves.

00:12:45.222 --> 00:12:46.599
That's my

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:50.436
of Who's an advocate for accessibility
based in Toronto.

00:12:50.436 --> 00:12:52.938
And I think she really nailed it
on the head.

00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:57.193
I mean, first, it needs to be functional
and it needs to be accessible,

00:12:57.568 --> 00:12:59.862
but that's kind of the bare minimum.

00:13:00.196 --> 00:13:03.991
Like once you figure that out,
how can we create environments

00:13:03.991 --> 00:13:07.244
that give everyone
the same quality of experience

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:11.040
and the same quality of life
and when you start

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.627
looking at the environment
from that lens, you're going to start

00:13:14.627 --> 00:13:18.714
to see a lot of problems
in a lot of standard design practice

00:13:18.714 --> 00:13:24.011
and in a lot of like award winning spaces
by a lot of award winning architects.

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:27.431
But also, if you're smart, I think

00:13:27.473 --> 00:13:30.601
you'll also start
to see more opportunities in this.

00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:32.645
If you think architecture is stagnant.

00:13:32.937 --> 00:13:36.398
This is one of the ways
we're going to get new types of spaces.

00:13:36.398 --> 00:13:37.900
And I think it's something that all of us

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:41.320
can do kind of on a daily basis
if you're going somewhere.

00:13:41.821 --> 00:13:44.532
Just try to think
if I was on a wheelchair,

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:48.118
like how would I get here
if I was visually impaired?

00:13:48.285 --> 00:13:49.245
How would I do that?

00:13:49.245 --> 00:13:54.458
And by understanding and recognizing that,
I think we can develop

00:13:54.458 --> 00:13:58.295
more empathy and we can have better
and stronger communities.

00:13:58.504 --> 00:14:00.881
So where am I going with all of this?

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:06.720
As an architect, I do want to attribute
the success of Burning Man to the design.

00:14:07.304 --> 00:14:11.100
But I am it's not I think it's actually

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:15.062
the social strategy behind their design
and the urban plan.

00:14:15.187 --> 00:14:19.733
This is a quote from Larry Harvey, who's
one of the co-founders of Burning Man.

00:14:19.942 --> 00:14:23.946
He said, We've shown that
you can actually deal with the complexity

00:14:23.946 --> 00:14:27.908
of urban problems
by using specifically cultural means.

00:14:27.908 --> 00:14:30.494
The citizens participate
in creating the city.

00:14:30.536 --> 00:14:33.664
In fact, half of our control is just based

00:14:33.664 --> 00:14:36.667
on watching their behavior
and meeting their needs.

00:14:36.667 --> 00:14:40.212
And that's the whole history
of the development of the city.

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:43.966
So what is participation on one level?

00:14:44.174 --> 00:14:48.095
Participation
means that people should enter up

00:14:48.178 --> 00:14:53.642
with others and engage in the activities
and not be voyeuristic.

00:14:53.684 --> 00:14:57.271
On a deeper level, participation
means that aside

00:14:57.271 --> 00:15:01.150
from the limited government
that handles like the basic infrastructure

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:05.696
and like emergency services,
the citizens they co-create

00:15:05.821 --> 00:15:10.075
and they maintain the city
and essentially it's civic engagement.

00:15:10.117 --> 00:15:14.413
I think there's something kind of magical
that comes from the process

00:15:14.413 --> 00:15:15.998
of building something together.

00:15:15.998 --> 00:15:19.376
When I interviewed Scott Kemp
and some other architects

00:15:19.376 --> 00:15:22.463
who work with indigenous communities,
a lot of them,

00:15:22.546 --> 00:15:26.383
they don't see architecture
or the building as their end goal.

00:15:26.675 --> 00:15:30.763
Their goal is to use the process
of building

00:15:31.138 --> 00:15:34.558
as a way to heal the communities
and to bring them together.

00:15:34.642 --> 00:15:38.979
And in fact, Scott
went as far as to say that he's not really

00:15:38.979 --> 00:15:42.524
the one who designs the buildings,
like the community designed them,

00:15:42.691 --> 00:15:46.445
and he's just the hand
that draws what they design.

00:15:46.528 --> 00:15:49.823
Even if you're contributing
at a really small level,

00:15:50.074 --> 00:15:54.453
like gifting someone a cookie
or saying hi to your neighbors,

00:15:54.703 --> 00:15:59.166
you are participating
in building the social fabric of the city.

00:15:59.291 --> 00:16:02.795
And if you don't like something,
you can talk to someone.

00:16:02.836 --> 00:16:06.715
In normal cities,
there's just like so many steps

00:16:06.966 --> 00:16:10.761
you have to get through bureaucracies
and there's political barriers.

00:16:11.136 --> 00:16:16.016
And eventually
people feel pretty powerless in the end.

00:16:16.058 --> 00:16:20.562
But in essence, I think it's
the principle of participation

00:16:20.771 --> 00:16:25.401
that's allowed Burning Men
to transform from this to this.

00:16:25.609 --> 00:16:29.113
And I think this is how they've been able
to continue growing through

00:16:29.238 --> 00:16:32.616
so many years of conflict
with the authorities.

00:16:32.658 --> 00:16:37.204
I do wonder if going to Burning Man
actually affects people's attitudes

00:16:37.204 --> 00:16:40.749
when they leave the event
and come back to their normal lives.

00:16:40.749 --> 00:16:45.629
But I mean, I would kind of assume
that if you've had

00:16:45.629 --> 00:16:50.384
a really positive experience somewhere,
it kind of changes a little part of you.

00:16:51.051 --> 00:16:51.969
I don't know.

